details.image_prompt:
[
  {
    "prompt": "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The composition features two matte ceramic plates placed on a warm, aged oak table with visible grain and small nicks. The primary plate is centered in the frame at a 3/4 overhead angle, ensuring all contents of both the stuffed portobello and the quinoa are clearly visible. The second plate is positioned slightly behind and blurred, maintaining a shallow depth of field. On each plate, two large, roasted portobello caps are filled with a hearty mixture of mashed and whole cannellini beans, wilted spinach, and robust red marinara sauce, topped with bubbly, golden-brown melted mozzarella. Beside the mushrooms on each plate sits a neat mound of fluffy, light-colored quinoa. Soft natural side light from a window to the left illuminates the textures, highlighting the porous surface of the roasted mushroom caps, the vibrancy of the spinach, and the glistening sheen of the melted cheese and sauce. Props include a rumpled, off-white linen napkin tucked under one side of the plate and a wooden spoon resting on the table surface, along with a few stray grains of quinoa and a tiny spinach leaf scattered near the plates. Photorealistic, not AI-generated looking. The scene should look like someone just finished cooking and plating — lived-in, warm, authentic. Slight natural imperfections: an asymmetric garnish placement, a small sauce drip on the plate rim, slightly uneven browning on the cheese. Include subtle traces of the cooking process: a crumb trail on the surface, a slight oil mark near the plate. No plastic sheen, no unnaturally smooth surfaces, no perfect symmetry. Warm, golden-hour color temperature. No text, no watermark, no logo, no hands, no faces. The image should be indistinguishable from a photograph taken by a professional food photographer for a printed cookbook."
  }
]
